// ============================================================================
// sum-of-primes-below.epher -- the sum of the primes below n
//
// Adds the primes below a bound with a packed while loop (guide 1.30):
// the state holds {candidate, running sum}, the loop is silent, and one
// labelled print shows the total. The check value is the classic one: the
// primes below 100 add to 1060 (Hardy and Wright, ch. 1, exercise).
//
// Source: standard number theory; the result is exact because isprime is
// exact and the sums stay far below the calculator's exact range.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/primes/sum-of-primes-below.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/primes/sum-of-primes-below.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// The one knob to edit is `bound` below.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
bound = 100

// ---- sum the primes below `bound` ----------------------------------------------
// v holds {candidate, sum}; each pass adds the candidate when it is prime
// and moves on, so the loop stops with v[2] = sum of the primes below it.
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] < bound do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1]) then v[1] else 0)}
print("sum of primes below " + str(bound) + ":", v[2])

// ---- the same sum for a larger bound -------------------------------------------
bound = 1000
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] < bound do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1]) then v[1] else 0)}
print("sum of primes below " + str(bound) + ":", v[2])
// ---- expected output ----
// = 100
// = {2, 0}
// = sum of primes below 100: 1060
// = 1000
// = {2, 0}
// = sum of primes below 1000: 76127
